   "subject": "Re: boxplot + jitter",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Here&#39;s a toy example to illustrate how you could do something like this. The key is to break the data up into two data frames, one of which is used to produce the boxplots in one layer and the other to produce the jittered stripplots in a second layer. Also notice that the levels of the grouping factor are the same in both data frames.<div>\r\n<br></div><div><div>DF1 &lt;- data.frame(gp = factor(rep(LETTERS[1:2], each = 50),</div><div>                              levels = LETTERS[1:4]),</div><div>                   y = rnorm(100))</div><div><br></div><div>DF2 &lt;- data.frame(gp = factor(rep(LETTERS[3:4], each = 5),</div>\r\n<div>                              levels = LETTERS[1:4]),</div><div>                   y = rnorm(10))</div><div><br></div><div>library(ggplot2)</div><div><br></div><div>ggplot(DF1, aes(x = gp, y = y)) +</div><div>    geom_boxplot() +</div>\r\n<div>    geom_point(data = DF2, position = position_jitter(width = 0.3))</div></div><div><br></div><div>An alternative is to use geom_dotplot() in place of geom_point():</div><div><br></div><div><div>ggplot(DF1, aes(x = gp, y = y)) +</div>\r\n<div>    geom_boxplot() +</div><div>    geom_dotplot(data = DF2)</div></div><div><br></div><div>Dennis</div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Fri, Jun 20, 2014 at 11:53 AM, Kristof Haneca <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">kristof...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n</div></div>"
